<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Lint's severities for this app, and the reason for each one. `lintDebug` must end at
    0 errors (docs/PLAN.md §8 gate_static #8), so nothing here is set to `error` unless the
    thing it forbids is genuinely absent today — every `error` below is a tripwire for a
    change nobody has made yet, not a note about existing code.

    The three groups, in order: the one default that is wrong for this project's locale
    layout, the security and power invariants that STANDARDS §10 states in prose and this
    file states in a form the build can check, and the two checks that fire on things that
    are deliberate here.
-->
<lint>

    <!--
        values/ is ALREADY Simplified Chinese (docs/PLAN.md D17 — Fred reads Chinese, so his
        language is the default), values-en/ carries the English, and values-zh-rCN/ exists
        to override exactly ONE string: the launcher name, which STANDARDS §1.6 wants as
        日本美食地图 on a zh-CN device while everyone else sees "Japan Foodmap". Lint sees
        that one-string folder and calls every other string "not translated in zh" — but a
        zh-CN device already resolves them from values/. Copying the whole table into
        values-zh-rCN to satisfy the check would create two Chinese copies to keep in step,
        which is a real bug source traded for a false one.

        A WARNING rather than `ignore`, so a genuinely missing values-en/ string still shows
        up. This duplicates `lint { warning += "MissingTranslation" }` in app/build.gradle.kts
        on purpose: the Gradle block is what T1 shipped and app/build.gradle.kts is not this
        task's file to edit, and a severity stated in both places cannot drift into an error
        if one of them is ever removed.
    -->
    <issue id="MissingTranslation" severity="warning" />

    <!-- ======================= security / power invariants ======================= -->

    <!--
        The bridge is addWebMessageListener, origin-scoped to https://jpfoodmap.com, and
        nothing else (STANDARDS §10.6). addJavascriptInterface hands a Java object to every
        frame the WebView ever loads, which for an app whose whole content is remote is the
        difference between "a page can call four named things" and "a page can call
        anything". tools/static-audit.sh greps for it too; this is the half that runs inside
        the build.
    -->
    <issue id="AddJavascriptInterface" severity="error" />

    <!--
        Power. None of these exist in this app and none may: STANDARDS §10.1 is "no
        WorkManager, no Service, no AlarmManager, no JobScheduler, no wakelock, no FCM, no
        background network", and a wakelock is the one of those a plausible future edit
        ("keep the screen on while navigating") reaches for first.
    -->
    <issue id="Wakelock" severity="error" />
    <issue id="WakelockTimeout" severity="error" />
    <issue id="InvalidWakeLockTag" severity="error" />
    <issue id="BatteryLife" severity="error" />

    <!--
        Permissions. The manifest declares four and the audit asserts four (STANDARDS §10.3).
        QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES is the one a "why can't I see whether Chrome is installed"
        debugging session ends at; the <queries> block in the manifest is the answer instead.
        ScopedStorage covers the legacy-storage flags, which is the other easy regression.
    -->
    <issue id="QueryAllPackagesPermission" severity="error" />
    <issue id="ScopedStorage" severity="error" />
    <issue id="ProtectedPermissions" severity="error" />

    <!-- Backup and transfer are off, and the WebView data directory holds a 90-day session
         cookie (res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml). Both of these fire if that stops
         being true. -->
    <issue id="AllowBackup" severity="error" />
    <issue id="DataExtractionRules" severity="error" />

    <!-- An implicit intent built out of one this app received is how a deep link becomes a
         way to launch something else. T4 parses intents; this is the check that watches it. -->
    <issue id="UnsafeIntentLaunch" severity="error" />
    <issue id="UnsafeProtectedBroadcastReceiver" severity="error" />

    <!-- targetSdk is the contract the whole shell is written against: edge-to-edge, and
         predictive back (STANDARDS §1.2, §4.1). Lowering it silently would take both away. -->
    <issue id="OldTargetApi" severity="error" />
    <issue id="ExpiredTargetSdkVersion" severity="error" />

    <!-- ======================= deliberate, so quiet ======================= -->

    <!--
        JavaScript IS the app. jpfoodmap.com is a Leaflet map; a WebView with JavaScript off
        shows a blank page. The risk lint is pointing at is answered by the settings around
        it instead — file access off, content access off, mixed content never, no
        addJavascriptInterface, and a network security config that refuses cleartext — all of
        which tools/static-audit.sh asserts on every run.
    -->
    <issue id="SetJavaScriptEnabled" severity="ignore" />

    <!--
        There is no Play listing and never will be: this APK is sideloaded from Dropbox onto
        one phone (STANDARDS §14.7). App-indexing metadata would be markup for a search
        result nobody will ever see.
    -->
    <issue id="GoogleAppIndexingWarning" severity="ignore" />

</lint>
